        {
          "slug": "house-dance",
          "name": "House dance",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The club dance that grew up with house music in Chicago and New York in the 1980s.",
          "description": "House dance was born on the floors of underground clubs — The Warehouse in Chicago, where the music got its name, and New York's Loft and Paradise Garage. Dancers pulled in whatever they already knew: tap and jazz footwork, salsa and Latin steps, breaking and capoeira floorwork, African and Caribbean grooves, and the swing of Lindy Hop. What holds it all together is the music — a steady four-on-the-floor kick at roughly 118 to 128 BPM — and the idea that the dancer never stops riding it. It is overwhelmingly a freestyle dance: people learn vocabulary so they can improvise with it, not to repeat set routines.",
          "tips": [
            "Think of house as three elements that mix freely: jacking (the groove), footwork, and lofting (the floor).",
            "Every step on this page is meant to be done on top of a running bounce or jack. If the groove stops, it is no longer house."
          ],
          "related": ["jacking", "footwork", "lofting", "on-the-one"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "jacking",
          "name": "Jacking",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The groove element: a continuous wave through the torso that rides the kick drum.",
          "description": "Jacking is the heartbeat of house — the upper body rocking forward, back, side to side or in circles on every beat. It comes from Chicago, where tracks literally told dancers to \"jack your body\". Jacking is what keeps you connected to the music while your feet are busy, and what you fall back on when you run out of ideas. Every other element sits on top of it.",
          "tips": [
            "A good jack is visible from across the room but costs almost no effort — it is a release, not a push.",
            "Practise jacking for a whole track without footwork. It builds the stamina the rest of the dance needs."
          ],
          "related": ["jack", "side-jack", "rolling-jack", "bounce"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "footwork",
          "name": "Footwork",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The element people picture first: fast, light, rhythmic steps on the balls of the feet.",
          "description": "House footwork is dense, quick and grounded, borrowing from tap, jazz, salsa and Latin social dance. Most steps live on the balls of the feet so they can change direction instantly, and most are short — two, four or eight counts — so they can be chained into longer phrases on the fly. The goal is not to collect steps but to be able to move between them without a visible seam.",
          "tips": [
            "Learn each step slowly on the spot, then at tempo, then travelling, then out of and into another step.",
            "Keep the heels light and the knees soft. Locked knees make every step look heavy and slow you down."
          ],
          "related": ["heel-toe", "toe-twist", "loose-legs", "shuffle"]
        },
        {
          "slug": "lofting",
          "name": "Lofting",
          "learnable": false,
          "summary": "The floor element: drops, spins, rolls and glides that take the dance to the ground and back.",
          "description": "Lofting is named after The Loft, David Mancuso's New York party, and covers everything that takes a house dancer down to the floor and back up. It borrows from breaking, capoeira and contemporary dance, but the priority is different: lofting should flow in and out of footwork without breaking the groove, and it should look light — the dancer seems to float down and bounce back rather than crash. Good lofting is as much about the entry and exit as the move itself.",
          "tips": [
            "Learn to get down and up smoothly before learning anything that happens on the floor.",
            "Protect knees, wrists and the neck. Land on muscle — thighs, shins, shoulder blades — never on bone or the kneecap."
          ],
          "related": ["knee-drop", "back-roll", "seat-spin", "spiral", "kip-up"]
        }
      ]
