          "description": "The pas de bourrée comes from ballet and jazz, where it links other steps together, and house uses it the same way but grounded and bouncing. The classic version steps behind, to the side, then in front, changing weight on each, over \"1 and 2\". It is invaluable for changing which foot is free without breaking the rhythm.",
